# Generated from: specs/388-support-package-scoped-publication-only-upgrades/requirements.md
Feature: Support package-scoped publication-only upgrades
  Publication repair must represent the operator's exact package authority and
  must not inherit the full repository upgrade's unrelated mutations.

  @SCN001 @regression
  Scenario: Detect exactly one explicitly selected package
    Given several spec packages contain publication rewrites or findings
    When publication-only detection receives exactly one valid package
    Then the report includes only that package
    And zero repeated or multiple selections fail before detection

  @SCN002 @regression
  Scenario: Approval binds exact single-package authority
    Given one selected publication-only package
    When its approval id is calculated
    Then it binds canonical root package and complete locale-independent inventory
    And it binds exact-string issue digits, regular-file digests/identities, and exact rewrite/finding content

  @SCN003 @regression
  Scenario: Apply performs only selected publication writes
    Given one selected package has approved rewrites and unselected packages are dirty
    When publication-only apply receives the exact approval id and selection
    Then only selected File(s) line payloads change
    And no dependency label backfill GitHub or other upgrade side effect runs

  @SCN004 @regression
  Scenario Outline: Invalid selection fails closed
    Given publication-only detection receives <selection>
    When it validates package authority
    Then it fails before mutation with a stable reason code
    Examples:
      | selection |
      | an empty set |
      | a duplicate path |
      | an absolute or traversal path |
      | a path outside specs |
      | a root whose final component is a symlink |
      | a root with a symlinked ancestor |
      | a symlink hidden lexically before dot-dot |
      | a missing package |
      | a symlinked package |
      | an incomplete package |
      | a wrongly named package |
      | an issue-mismatched package |
      | a non-Approved package |

  @SCN005 @regression
  Scenario Outline: Stale foreign or interrupted apply fails closed
    Given an approved single-package report and exclusive root lock
    When apply observes <change>
    Then unapproved bytes never install
    And foreign lock or staged identities are never overwritten or deleted
    Examples:
      | change |
      | changed source bytes |
      | added package content after staging |
      | same-byte target identity replacement |
      | staged identity replacement |
      | staged byte mutation |
      | duplicate task declarations |
      | a different package root or report id |
      | a pre-existing foreign lock |
      | a partial or wrong-byte owner write |
      | a same-token foreign lock replacement |
      | an atomic rename failure |

  @SCN006 @regression
  Scenario: Mixed line endings and arbitrary unrelated bytes are preserved
    Given a selected tasks file mixes CRLF and LF and contains invalid UTF-8 byte 0xff
    When publication-only apply succeeds
    Then every original line separator remains in place
    And only approved ASCII declaration-label spans differ
    And byte 0xff and all unrelated files remain byte-for-byte equal

  @SCN007 @regression
  Scenario: PathCast-shaped four-token repair converges
    Given selected T001 through T004 contain four recoverable PathCast-style Files declarations
    And unselected packages contain rewrites and findings
    When the selected item is approved and applied
    Then exactly four selected tokens become canonical File(s) declarations
    And repeating selected detection reports zero writes
    And every unselected package remains unchanged

  @SCN008 @regression
  Scenario: Workflow uses an unambiguous dedicated native entry point
    Given package-bounded publication authority in /sdlc-upgrade-project
    When the workflow renders detection and apply commands
    Then it uses detect-publication and apply-publication with exactly one command token and one spec option
    And repeated specs ambiguous options values positionals and extra commands fail before mutation
    And existing unbounded detect and apply commands retain their parsing semantics

  @SCN009 @regression
  Scenario: Applied cleanup failure remains truthful
    Given the one staged tasks output has atomically replaced its target
    When exact owned lock unlink returns EACCES
    Then the stable outcome is publication_files_cleanup_failed
    And state is applied_cleanup_failed with applied true
    And the valid root lock remains as ownership evidence
